<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">League of Mayapan</h1><p class="st-meta">1220 CE – 1441 CE (approx.) · capital Mayapan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The confederation of Maya lineages that ruled northern Yucatán from the walled city of Mayapan between the fall of Chichen Itza and the 1440s.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the decline of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chichen-itza">Chichen Itza</a>, Mayapan was founded or expanded around 1220 in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula">Yucatán</a>. Colonial Maya chronicles, the books of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/books-of-chilam-balam">Chilam Balam</a>, say that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cocom">Cocom</a> lineage, aided by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-coast-of-mexico">Gulf Coast</a>, took power under the leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunac-ceel">Hunac Ceel</a>, though these accounts are partly legendary.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Mayapan was a densely packed walled city of about 15,000 people, with a smaller copy of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chichen-itza">Chichen Itza</a>'s Castillo pyramid. Leading lords of the provinces were required to live in the city, and power was shared among noble lineages headed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cocom">Cocom</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutul-xiu">Xiu</a> and others. It received tribute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, honey and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> and made many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> burners for household cults.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 1441 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutul-xiu">Xiu</a> led a revolt, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cocom">Cocom</a> were massacred and the city was sacked and abandoned. Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula">Yucatán</a> split into some sixteen rival provinces, including <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mani">Maní</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sotuta">Sotuta</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ah-kin-chel">Ah Kin Chel</a>, which the Spanish later conquered.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/league-of-mayapan.jpg" alt="League of Mayapan" width="320" height="252"><figcaption>Image: Mabarlabin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mayapan_1200.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1220 CE – 1441 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mayapan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maya religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Yucatec Maya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mayapan League, Mayapan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1330&amp;focus=league-of-mayapan" aria-label="League of Mayapan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2271 1414 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where League of Mayapan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2591 1670L2616 1668M2591 1670L2592 1679M2591 1670L2603 1672M2591 1670L2574 1680M2591 1670L2600 1654M2591 1670L2599 1664M2591 1670L2592 1666M2591 1670L2618 1686M2591 1670L2563 1677M2591 1670L2608 1748M2591 1670L2548 1716M2591 1670L2658 1678"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2616 1668h0M2592 1679h0M2603 1672h0M2574 1680h0M2600 1654h0M2599 1664h0M2592 1666h0M2618 1686h0M2563 1677h0M2608 1748h0M2548 1716h0M2658 1678h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2591 1670h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2591 1670h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mayapan. <a href="/atlas?year=1330&amp;focus=league-of-mayapan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
